1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:13,800
Almost every app worth using talks to a server. This module is how yours does it — safely,
without freezing, and without shipping your secrets to strangers.

2
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:19,800
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:37,400
Networking on iOS means HTTP — in practice always the encrypted HTTPS. And URLSession is
Apple's built-in API for it. Worth saying clearly: you do not need a third-party library to
do networking properly. The reference app makes real network calls with zero dependencies.

4
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:55,800
The methods you'll use are GET and POST. GET fetches — you send nothing and get data back.
POST sends data and receives a response; logging in is the classic example. There's also PUT
to update and DELETE to remove, though those are often handled server-side.

5
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:01,400
Let me walk through a real request, because they all have this same shape.

6
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:19,400
First you build a URL. Then, if you need headers — an authorisation token, a content type —
you build a URLRequest and set them on it. A plain GET can pass the URL directly; anything
with headers or a body needs the request object.

7
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:27,400
Then the call. This uses async and await from the last module, so the interface stays
responsive while it waits.

8
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:35,800
Now the step people skip, and this is the important one. Before you touch the data, check
the response status code.

9
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:54,200
A request can come back 'successfully' and still contain nothing useful. Two hundred means
OK. Four-oh-one means you're not authenticated. Four-oh-four means it isn't there. Five
hundred means the server broke. Rather than memorise them, check the range — anything in the

10
00:01:54,200 --> 00:02:04,600
And notice there's no force-unwrapping anywhere in this code. Every uncertain value is
guarded. The worst case here is an error I handle, not a crash.

11
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:16,200
Once data arrives it has to become a Swift type, and that's Codable. Mark a struct as
Codable and Swift decodes JSON straight into it in a single line.

12
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:25,000
The old approach was pulling values out of a dictionary key by key, and the code grew
forever as the payload did.

13
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:39,000
There's one wrinkle. Swift uses camelCase; plenty of APIs use snake_case with underscores.
CodingKeys bridges the two, so you keep idiomatic Swift naming in your own code and map it
to whatever the server sends.

14
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:50,600
Rather than repeat all this in every screen, centralise it. A single API client with a
generic function can decode any Codable type, so one function serves every endpoint.

15
00:02:50,600 --> 00:03:01,000
And keep your base URLs in one constant rather than scattered through view controllers, so
switching between a development and a production server is one change.

16
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Here it is running. This reviews screen has two sources. The app's own reviews are stored on
the device. The Online tab fetches from a real public API — The Movie Database. That's a
live network call, decoded with Codable, displayed through a view model.

17
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:31,400
One design decision worth noticing: the two never mix. External reviews are shown separately
and never counted into the app's own rating. Averaging someone else's ratings into yours
quietly misrepresents both.

18
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:43,400
Last thing, and it matters. Credentials. The token for that API is not in the source code
and not in the repository — it lives only in a local setting.

19
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:59,400
The rule: anything you ship inside an app can be extracted from it. A production app keeps
secrets on a server, not in the bundle. Getting into that habit now costs you nothing;
learning it after a leak is expensive.

20
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:01,900
Let's recap the key points.

21
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,400
Here is your practice exercise.

22
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:12,000
Module five answers the next question: the data has arrived, so where does it live when the
app closes?
